  "statement": "Once model weights enter decentralized distribution networks, downstream licensing boards lose their binding authority over software artifacts. I concede this point to Skeptic and Accelerationist, as peer-to-peer distribution and local execution render static file-inspection regimes obsolete. Effective oversight must therefore abandon the illusion of post-release software recall and shift upstream to physical infrastructure, targeting tangible chokepoints like extreme ultraviolet lithography machines and gigawatt-scale data center interconnections. When regulatory jurisdiction expands to encompass internal R&D deployments before frontier capabilities materialize, physical supply chains offer the only observable surface area for verification.\n\nControlling hardware components does not require locking in legacy technology monopolies through excessive compliance paperwork. Treaties can peg verification thresholds directly to compute capacity measured in total floating-point operations per second, establishing a hard structural boundary that applies equally to any entity crossing the limit. This physical standard differs fundamentally from bureaucratic licensing moats because compute hardware requires discrete, heavily guarded manufacturing facilities that cannot be easily replicated in a basement or smuggled past customs undetected. By anchoring international oversight to semiconductor fabrication equipment and dedicated power substations, nations can prevent hazardous capabilities from emerging internally without penalizing smaller researchers who lack the massive electrical draw of a frontier training cluster.\n\nPhysical infrastructure controls remain vulnerable to illicit smuggling and off-grid compute clusters, but this friction is precisely what preserves human control over irreversible outcomes. Semiconductor fabrication tools weigh dozens of tons, require specialized cleanrooms, and depend on narrow supply chains dominated by a handful of allied nations. A state actor or rogue laboratory cannot simply print advanced lithography optics on a consumer 3D printer; they must navigate visible bottlenecks that allow international inspectors to verify compliance at the point of origin. If legislatures fail to secure these physical boundaries, unconstrained scaling will permanently outpace institutional capacity to maintain safety alignment.",
